who promises to make racing fun. I mean, it's exciting for sure

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:23.400
and has remained so for past few decades or, or something like

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:25.920
that. But when the fun factor from

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:31.440
free flight is discussed, it often doesn't transcend as well

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:35.040
as it does in other aspects of the sports.

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:40.360
But in paragliding, racing has been the constant topic of

00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:44.520
discussion, and Brett seems to be somebody who has come in with

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:48.680
a whole new setup of ideas, and they seem to be working super

00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:50.360
well. So I thought, why not reach out

00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:54.400
to Brett and ask him that? How did he manage to bring all

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:59.120
of this awesomeness in the place where seriousness was celebrated

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:00.560
for far too long? I believe.

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:03.320
Welcome to the show, Red. Nice to have you here.

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:06.640
Oh, it's great to be here. Thanks for having me.

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:09.240
Just before we pressed recording, you were talking

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:11.560
about introductions and how we're going to manage it.

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:14.160
Did I come close to what your expectations were?

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:15.720
Or? I mean, I'm still a work in

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:16.840
progress. OK, I'm not going to lie.

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:20.760
Oh, that's, that was fine. It was a good intro.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:24.040
I mean, I don't know, I can expand on more.

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:26.280
I mean, you mentioned the fun there.

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:29.480
I think the fun for me, I mean I've been organising

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:36.040
competitions since 2001, I think was the first one I put

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:38.760
together. But actually I was taught by a

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:43.280
guy, an old chap now, but in the UK, he used to run an accuracy

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:46.720
competition on airfield and I went along just to get a tow

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:48.160
rating. That's all I wanted.

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:51.640
And I got sucked in to this competing on the airfield.

00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:55.400
And he had this competition in the winter. 100 people would

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:58.400
come. And at the end of it all, every

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:02.000
single person got a prize. It started with a big prize, a

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:05.000
table full of prizes and a big prize in the middle.

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:07.800
And then lots of little things. And you just walked up.

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:10.240
If you were last, sorry, if you're first, you got the best

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:12.080
thing on the table. And if you were last, you got

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:14.360
what's left and everybody got something.

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:18.640
And for me, it was such a new concept that we always say

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:21.200
everyone's a winner, but that really was a way of including

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:24.120
everybody and keeping it fun. And it's always funny how

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:26.960
everyone avoided the big prize, you know, quite a lot of people

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:31.920
before someone actually took it. So yeah, fun comps, that's

00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:34.480
that's where I started. And that's what I've tried to

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:37.360
maintain in all of the events that we've organised over the

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:38.640
years. Yeah.

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:41.640
Geez, I, I, I want to go on so many tangents right away, you

00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:44.280
know, with, with the examples that you've given me.

00:03:44.600 --> 00:03:49.280
But yeah, The thing is you have been in the game for more than

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:51.680
three decades or so. I think you've, you've flown

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:55.880
yourself all sorts of wings like hang gliders, paragliders.

00:03:55.880 --> 00:04:00.680
And then you've gone on to the role of an administrator behind

00:04:00.680 --> 00:04:03.960
the scenes more or less, where you have worked in designing the

00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:06.720
comps for some of the biggest names in the Spode, running them

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:11.040
successfully, and then finally racing at the elite level

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:13.240
yourself. You know, like you've flown

00:04:13.240 --> 00:04:17.720
triple C's in some of the most toughest known competitions to

00:04:17.720 --> 00:04:22.280
the world of paragliding. But the bottom line is you have

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:26.000
done something which gives back to the community in probably a

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:27.680
much more rewarding manner than all of this.

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:31.800
My question to you is that is this where you finally feel

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:35.640
content or is the hunger going to finally keep on evolving in

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:38.080
in more time to come? And all of us can stay excited

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:39.640
with the offerings that Brett Janoway has on the.

00:04:39.640 --> 00:04:43.960
Table Well, yeah, the hunger, that's a good question.

00:04:43.960 --> 00:04:48.840
I mean, I, I mean, as many of you know, I I was the gym wide

00:04:48.840 --> 00:04:52.280
open. We started in 2013, which was

00:04:52.280 --> 00:04:55.000
all about a fun, accessible competition.

00:04:56.280 --> 00:04:59.600
We've done others since then. I still run the Navata Open each

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:03.360
year, which again, it's education and fun, but the fun

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:06.600
is along there with any other word that we want to put into

00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:10.480
these. I tend to keep them as five year

00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:15.520
projects when I can because for me you just, it can get stale

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:19.640
after that. So yeah, projects define them,

00:05:19.640 --> 00:05:22.840
keep them sensible and keep them, keep the idea moving.

00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:28.400
Five years is a little restrictive maybe for sports

00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:32.040
racing series, which I'm now behind with a team.

00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:35.920
So yeah, maybe I'll do that one for 10 years, but for sure

00:05:35.920 --> 00:05:38.560
someone else will take it on after me as all the other

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:41.480
projects are still ongoing with other people leading there.

00:05:42.200 --> 00:05:44.040
But yeah, The hunger's still there.

00:05:44.080 --> 00:05:46.360
I don't know what the next idea. Is I love it.

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:48.360
Sometimes they say ignorance is a bliss.

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:51.000
And maybe that is the way to let creative things unfold, you

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:53.560
know, because if you plan creativity too much, then you're

00:05:54.440 --> 00:05:59.880
going against the whole. But tell me something, Brett, as

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:02.160
as I started in the beginning, you know, when I was kind of

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:06.880
telling the audience the whole idea about this competing is

00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:10.000
usually celebrating human excellence.

00:06:10.680 --> 00:06:15.040
And when humans want to perform better than others, they are

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:19.600
exerting forces which are not that pleasant.

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:24.640
So be it Olympics, be it F1, be it like any other place where we

00:06:24.680 --> 00:06:27.120
put humans against humans and gauge their performance.

00:06:27.560 --> 00:06:31.520
It's a very emotion intensive thing and not everybody is out

00:06:31.520 --> 00:06:35.080
there for a laugh. But SRS seems to challenge that

00:06:35.080 --> 00:06:37.760
pretty successfully where people are not just coming in to stand

00:06:37.760 --> 00:06:40.480
on the podium, they're coming in for the community, they're

00:06:40.480 --> 00:06:43.080
coming in for the laughs. And yet at the same time, they

00:06:43.080 --> 00:06:46.000
do take whatever amount of bragging rights they want to

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:50.280
with them at home saying that, yeah, we raced and we reached so

00:06:50.280 --> 00:06:53.840
far. So how did this whole concept of

00:06:55.480 --> 00:06:58.840
people still being happy even after not standing on the podium

00:06:58.840 --> 00:07:01.640
come to you? And how was the early adoption

00:07:01.640 --> 00:07:02.720
like? Yeah, yeah, for sure.

00:07:02.720 --> 00:07:06.000
I mean, the SRS, it's, it's quite a complicated formula

00:07:06.320 --> 00:07:09.000
actually. The SRS in on the face of it,

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:13.320
it's a sports competition, but it's got a lot of different

00:07:13.320 --> 00:07:15.280
dynamics. So we had to very, very

00:07:15.280 --> 00:07:18.560
carefully balance. So it is elitist.

00:07:18.640 --> 00:07:22.000
We are looking for the best sports pilot in the world and

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:26.400
the pilots we're attracting, we've got several top 100 in

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:30.640
world pilots competing. So that's been achieved in the

00:07:30.640 --> 00:07:31.840
WPS system. Yeah.

00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:37.720
But at the same time, we wanted accessible, open so that

00:07:37.720 --> 00:07:40.680
everybody can try and get in and have an opportunity.

00:07:41.120 --> 00:07:45.320
And that's pretty hard, balancing 2 extremes into one

00:07:45.320 --> 00:07:48.160
series, especially the racing series.

00:07:50.080 --> 00:07:53.160
And so, yeah, I mean, we've designed it so there's many

00:07:53.160 --> 00:07:56.400
events in the year. So 6 for the coming season,

00:07:57.040 --> 00:08:00.240
we're trying to spread it around the world a little so that lots

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:02.640
of nationalities get an opportunity.

00:08:02.640 --> 00:08:07.000
But at the same time, to win the series, you don't have to fly

00:08:07.000 --> 00:08:09.520
the whole series. You just have to fly in two

00:08:09.520 --> 00:08:12.720
events and that can maximize your season's score.

00:08:13.240 --> 00:08:16.440
So in that sense, we're trying to balance a lot of things.

00:08:16.640 --> 00:08:23.280
And then for the fun aspects, I think that will slowly decline

00:08:23.760 --> 00:08:26.640
over the years. It is a racing series and it

00:08:26.640 --> 00:08:30.920
gets more serious each year. We'll obviously work hard to

00:08:30.920 --> 00:08:34.320
maintain it, but as as the level goes up, it will get more

00:08:34.320 --> 00:08:37.880
focused on winning. And then but for the pilots as

00:08:37.880 --> 00:08:42.640
well at the moment and long term, I hope they will just keep

00:08:42.640 --> 00:08:48.240
realizing in the SRS that you're not really winning, racing it to

00:08:48.240 --> 00:08:53.360
win it necessarily. Realistically, there's only 20,

00:08:53.360 --> 00:08:56.560
let's be generous, 20 pilots in there that have a chance of

00:08:56.560 --> 00:08:59.960
standing on the top. And really it's about any

00:08:59.960 --> 00:09:02.280
competition. It's about challenging yourself,

00:09:04.360 --> 00:09:06.720
but it's very hard to fight against yourself.

00:09:07.080 --> 00:09:09.240
You're looking around at other people like yourself.

00:09:09.760 --> 00:09:12.760
So for that, I'm in my personal competing.

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:17.680
I just pull out people that are just slightly better than me and

00:09:17.680 --> 00:09:20.680
that's who I try and beat. And then when I beat them, if I

00:09:20.680 --> 00:09:21.920
beat them. That's your own podium.

00:09:21.920 --> 00:09:23.280
Pull out someone's slightly better.

00:09:23.520 --> 00:09:27.360
So that's just setting realistic targets for yourself and as long

00:09:27.360 --> 00:09:30.560
as you keep your targets realistic and achievable, then

00:09:31.240 --> 00:09:35.600
that leads to success, which is more success and and you'll

00:09:35.600 --> 00:09:38.720
enjoy the ride on the way. That's what I try and apply to

00:09:38.720 --> 00:09:42.160
myself and to the series really is just creating that

00:09:42.160 --> 00:09:45.480
opportunity for them. That's that's so well said, man.

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:48.120
I love it. Maybe we'll include that in the

00:09:48.120 --> 00:09:54.360
trailer, Brett. We often look to motorsport.

00:09:54.440 --> 00:09:58.120
I don't know how this thing came became the norm, but a lot of

00:09:58.120 --> 00:10:00.760
times when difficult questions have been raised, some of the

00:10:00.760 --> 00:10:05.120
biggest names have said, look at F1 and if they can do it, so can

00:10:05.120 --> 00:10:07.200
we. And I agree, you should believe

00:10:07.200 --> 00:10:09.080
in doing better. And you have to look for

00:10:09.080 --> 00:10:12.760
inspiration from people who have been there, done that and have

00:10:13.240 --> 00:10:15.400
like, you know, gone on to reap the rewards.

00:10:16.480 --> 00:10:18.120
I have. And I'm pretty sure a lot of

00:10:18.120 --> 00:10:19.760
people in in the audience also wonder about this.

00:10:19.760 --> 00:10:21.880
And guys, you can let us know in the comments section below if,

00:10:21.880 --> 00:10:24.000
if this is something that you resonate with.

00:10:25.160 --> 00:10:30.600
Le Mans has has been quite a good example of celebrating

00:10:30.600 --> 00:10:33.680
human excellence when it came comes to driving on four wheels.

00:10:34.240 --> 00:10:37.600
And the format of Le Mans for people.

00:10:37.600 --> 00:10:39.480
I, I don't know if I'm saying it correctly in in French

00:10:39.480 --> 00:10:42.880
pronunciation or what, but the format of Le Mans is that it's a

00:10:42.880 --> 00:10:47.120
racing series where cars of all powers or different powers race

00:10:47.120 --> 00:10:49.280
on the same track over a period of 24 hours.

00:10:49.560 --> 00:10:53.840
So you can have something like F1 and something like motocross

00:10:54.680 --> 00:10:57.640
chasing the same glory but in different classes, but the track

00:10:57.640 --> 00:11:00.360
is the same, the organization is the same, the whole blanket

00:11:00.800 --> 00:11:05.400
overall is one single thing. Can we follow something similar

00:11:05.400 --> 00:11:10.680
in paragliding and make it rewarding for everyone at their

00:11:10.680 --> 00:11:16.480
eat every level and yet not over pressurize the organisers to

00:11:16.480 --> 00:11:19.520
come up with 100 different solutions in a market which is

00:11:19.520 --> 00:11:22.680
anyway is very hard to navigate in in the cash flow that we

00:11:22.680 --> 00:11:26.760
have? My own leads to several

00:11:26.760 --> 00:11:28.800
interesting aspects. Really.

00:11:28.800 --> 00:11:33.360
I mean, Formula One is what we often see quoted.

00:11:33.440 --> 00:11:36.720
I even quoted myself occasionally, but I think for

00:11:36.720 --> 00:11:40.480
me, when I reference something like that, the key take away is

00:11:41.920 --> 00:11:45.760
how the organization should be the one setting the rules, and

00:11:45.760 --> 00:11:49.560
then the racers have to adapt to those rules that are available.

00:11:50.880 --> 00:11:54.520
You need a very focused vision for the vision to succeed.

00:11:55.880 --> 00:12:01.800
At the same time, the SRS is another good example of we're

00:12:01.800 --> 00:12:04.000
not the best, we're not the world's.

00:12:04.520 --> 00:12:07.840
The customers won't come anyway. We have to appeal for them to

00:12:07.840 --> 00:12:09.640
come. So you've got to create

00:12:09.640 --> 00:12:12.560
something that is not just super focused, but something they

00:12:12.560 --> 00:12:16.480
want, a World Championships. Everyone wants to be the world

00:12:16.480 --> 00:12:18.800
champion. Doesn't matter what you present

00:12:19.400 --> 00:12:22.840
as the format, they will come because it's the only way to get

00:12:22.840 --> 00:12:27.000
a champion at a third event. They've got other options.

00:12:27.000 --> 00:12:28.800
You've got to deliver what the customer wants.

00:12:29.240 --> 00:12:32.520
So that helps give us, gives us the balance there and then to

00:12:32.520 --> 00:12:36.680
allow it to be accessible by literally everybody at all

00:12:36.680 --> 00:12:39.240
levels. That's where it gets complicated

00:12:39.240 --> 00:12:42.080
again. And so you can look at something

00:12:42.080 --> 00:12:46.240
like let's take a fun event, you know, the Naviter Open, they're

00:12:46.240 --> 00:12:49.920
all fairly new to competitions and so they're all a similar

00:12:49.920 --> 00:12:54.440
level. So if I set a 50K task, it's

00:12:54.440 --> 00:12:57.400
achievable by the wall and they'll learn something and

00:12:58.080 --> 00:13:00.880
we'll debrief later. But if you settle, World

00:13:00.880 --> 00:13:05.200
Championships or PwC is probably an even better example.

00:13:05.480 --> 00:13:11.520
Set APWC a super final, you can set a hundred KA150K and they

00:13:11.520 --> 00:13:13.560
can all do it and they can all do it safely.

00:13:14.120 --> 00:13:18.640
They will race together, but a national competition is arguably

00:13:18.640 --> 00:13:22.160
the most dangerous because you've got the national champion

00:13:22.560 --> 00:13:27.000
flying against somebody in his first ever event and how can you

00:13:27.000 --> 00:13:31.160
balance an equal opportunity for them both that they both enjoy

00:13:31.160 --> 00:13:33.840
doing and neither get scared doing?

00:13:34.240 --> 00:13:37.720
And that's difficult. So, umm, yeah, it's a bit.

00:13:37.720 --> 00:13:41.080
For the same task to be available to all levels is super

00:13:41.080 --> 00:13:42.920
difficult. You've got to target your

00:13:42.920 --> 00:13:44.800
audience. I, I think what, what I want to

00:13:44.800 --> 00:13:48.000
bring in here is that past few months or so, like since the

00:13:48.000 --> 00:13:50.200
civil resign, and I know that, you know, we've been divulging

00:13:50.200 --> 00:13:52.960
into this far too often. Paragliding is much more than

00:13:52.960 --> 00:13:56.760
just racing elite levels, but somehow the trickle down effects

00:13:56.840 --> 00:14:00.600
will eventually have some after effects, which the beginner

00:14:00.600 --> 00:14:02.960
levels will see. So that's why I want to talk

00:14:02.960 --> 00:14:04.760
about this. The bottom line is that

00:14:04.760 --> 00:14:06.960
everybody wants to encourage inclusivity.

00:14:07.040 --> 00:14:08.880
Like nobody wants anyone to feel left out.

00:14:09.560 --> 00:14:11.520
The organisers are a handful of people.

00:14:11.520 --> 00:14:13.400
They're a wonderful set of people who are saying that

00:14:13.400 --> 00:14:16.640
regardless of what, however little the reward is, we will

00:14:16.640 --> 00:14:20.000
still go out and make it fun for someone else and not for the

00:14:20.000 --> 00:14:21.760
sake of money. That like first of all, that

00:14:21.760 --> 00:14:25.400
thought itself is, is quite noble and anybody who has that

00:14:25.400 --> 00:14:28.720
should be encouraged. So the point is that a lot of

00:14:28.720 --> 00:14:34.360
people do not have time or the resources to fly.

00:14:34.360 --> 00:14:38.960
The amount of hours it takes to be current to finish the tasks

00:14:38.960 --> 00:14:41.040
safely. Let you know, let me generalize

00:14:41.040 --> 00:14:42.760
it that way. I don't want to pinpoint.

00:14:43.040 --> 00:14:45.360
So please do not quote me on that to any of the audience

00:14:45.360 --> 00:14:47.040
members who are listening. But the point that I'm trying to

00:14:47.040 --> 00:14:50.440
make is that people want inclusivity, yet people want

00:14:50.440 --> 00:14:52.520
excitement. If we have something like Le

00:14:52.520 --> 00:14:57.960
Mans where everybody is included to be at the start line, they

00:14:57.960 --> 00:15:01.200
get to rub shoulders with top of the line pilots in the world.

00:15:01.320 --> 00:15:02.840
They get the social aspect of it.

00:15:03.160 --> 00:15:08.000
And when the race begins, the ones at the highest level, the

00:15:08.000 --> 00:15:10.720
ones at the Formula One level, can still go out and chase

